WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.703
A Torrance resident asks who approved a visible change near a major street.

2
00:00:04.703 --> 00:00:18.247
The planning commission discussed it, the City Council funded related work, a regional agency set part of the transportation framework, a private owner controlled the site, and state law limited what could be denied.

3
00:00:18.247 --> 00:00:22.574
The honest answer to "Who governs?" is often a network, not one name.

4
00:00:22.574 --> 00:00:26.399
This episode is advanced study preparation, not legal advice.

5
00:00:26.399 --> 00:00:30.224
It does not assign authority for any pending Torrance matter.

6
00:00:30.224 --> 00:00:34.864
Formal powers, agency relationships, and current officeholders can change.

7
00:00:34.864 --> 00:00:43.705
For a live decision, consult the current staff report, City Charter, Municipal Code, adopted budget, agency documents, and the city attorney.

8
00:00:43.705 --> 00:00:45.659
Start with formal institutions.

9
00:00:45.659 --> 00:00:50.008
Torrance uses a council-manager form of government under its charter.

10
00:00:50.008 --> 00:00:53.475
Voters elect officials as provided by current city law.

11
00:00:53.475 --> 00:01:00.031
The council legislates, adopts budgets, sets policy, and performs other charter and statutory functions.

12
00:01:00.031 --> 00:01:05.073
The city manager directs administration under the charter and council direction.

13
00:01:05.073 --> 00:01:08.729
Departments provide professional and operational capacity.

14
00:01:08.729 --> 00:01:11.755
Boards and commissions exercise delegated roles.

15
00:01:11.755 --> 00:01:14.906
The city attorney advises and represents the city.

16
00:01:14.906 --> 00:01:20.264
The city clerk performs legally significant election, records, and meeting functions.

17
00:01:20.264 --> 00:01:23.605
An organization chart is necessary, but insufficient.

18
00:01:23.605 --> 00:01:29.089
Formal authority tells you who may sign, vote, appoint, spend, regulate, or administer.

19
00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:41.128
Governance analysis also asks who sets the agenda, controls information, mobilizes constituencies, provides capital, owns land, supplies expertise, implements decisions, and can delay action.

20
00:01:41.128 --> 00:01:44.784
Those forms of influence do not all equal legal authority.

21
00:01:44.784 --> 00:01:56.697
MIT OpenCourseWare's Frameworks of Urban Governance encourages analysis of institutions, actors, resources, incentives, and scales rather than treating city hall as a single decision maker.

22
00:01:56.697 --> 00:01:59.282
Adapt that framework into five questions.

23
00:01:59.282 --> 00:02:00.857
Who has formal authority?

24
00:02:00.857 --> 00:02:02.874
Who controls critical resources?

25
00:02:02.874 --> 00:02:04.387
Who defines the problem?

26
00:02:04.387 --> 00:02:06.909
Who can block or reshape implementation?

27
00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:09.934
At what geographic scale does the issue operate?

28
00:02:09.934 --> 00:02:11.632
Consider elected officials.

29
00:02:11.632 --> 00:02:23.203
They possess democratic legitimacy and public decision power, but they face legal limits, budget constraints, electoral incentives, incomplete information, and implementation capacity.

30
00:02:23.203 --> 00:02:28.925
Professional staff contributes continuity, technical knowledge, and administrative control.

31
00:02:28.925 --> 00:02:31.315
Staff does not merely transmit orders.

32
00:02:31.315 --> 00:02:39.238
The design of options, timing, data selection, and feasibility analysis can shape what elected officials perceive as possible.

33
00:02:39.238 --> 00:02:46.595
Commissions add specialized public deliberation and can filter, develop, or decide matters under delegated authority.

34
00:02:46.595 --> 00:02:48.733
Their influence varies by subject.

35
00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:53.387
A recommendation can frame the council's choice even when it is not final.

36
00:02:53.387 --> 00:02:57.788
A final permit action can be legally significant even when appealable.

37
00:02:57.788 --> 00:03:05.649
A commission's power is strongest when members understand both their jurisdiction and the evidence needed for implementation.

38
00:03:05.649 --> 00:03:09.296
Voters govern directly as well as through representatives.

39
00:03:09.296 --> 00:03:10.994
Elections select officials.

40
00:03:10.994 --> 00:03:16.150
Initiatives and referenda can make or challenge local law within legal boundaries.

41
00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:19.232
Bond and tax measures may require voter approval.

42
00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:22.879
Public opinion affects agendas even without a formal vote.

43
00:03:22.879 --> 00:03:32.060
Yet the loudest organized constituency may differ from the full electorate, and the electorate may differ from all people affected by city policy.

44
00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:36.792
Private actors govern parts of urban life through ownership and investment.

45
00:03:36.792 --> 00:03:42.596
A city may zone land for housing, but owners decide whether and when to propose development.

46
00:03:42.596 --> 00:03:45.056
Lenders and insurers shape feasibility.

47
00:03:45.056 --> 00:03:48.715
Employers influence travel patterns and fiscal conditions.

48
00:03:48.715 --> 00:03:52.500
Business and neighborhood organizations aggregate interests.

49
00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:55.150
Nonprofits deliver services and knowledge.

50
00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:58.998
Utilities control infrastructure with their own legal duties.

51
00:03:58.998 --> 00:04:02.153
Media and digital networks frame public attention.

52
00:04:02.153 --> 00:04:07.326
Influence becomes power when an actor can change another actor's feasible choices.

53
00:04:07.326 --> 00:04:09.345
A landowner can withhold a site.

54
00:04:09.345 --> 00:04:11.553
A state agency can condition funds.

55
00:04:11.553 --> 00:04:13.635
Residents can appeal or litigate.

56
00:04:13.635 --> 00:04:17.231
Staff can identify that an option cannot be administered.

57
00:04:17.231 --> 00:04:18.997
A council can change zoning.

58
00:04:18.997 --> 00:04:20.890
A court can invalidate action.

59
00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:25.306
A regional body can allocate funds or establish planning requirements.

60
00:04:25.306 --> 00:04:26.820
Power is issue-specific.

61
00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:32.750
The actor who dominates a budget question may have little control over an adjudicative permit.

62
00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:36.283
Regional governance is especially important in Torrance.

63
00:04:36.283 --> 00:04:42.087
Air, housing markets, watersheds, transit, freight, jobs, and travel ignore city boundaries.

64
00:04:42.087 --> 00:04:58.616
Los Angeles County agencies, the Southern California Association of Governments, the South Coast Air Quality Management District, transportation agencies, school districts, special districts, neighboring cities, and state and federal agencies can shape outcomes.

65
00:04:58.616 --> 00:05:00.193
Their exact roles differ.

66
00:05:00.193 --> 00:05:06.060
Never refer vaguely to "the county" or "the region" when the responsible entity can be named.

67
00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:08.270
Time is another governing resource.

68
00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:16.098
A body that controls the agenda can sequence studies, budgets, and votes so that one option becomes feasible before another.

69
00:05:16.098 --> 00:05:22.853
Statutory deadlines can transfer leverage from local deliberation to applicants, courts, or state agencies.

70
00:05:22.853 --> 00:05:25.189
Map calendars alongside institutions.

71
00:05:25.189 --> 00:05:27.083
Fiscal structure also governs.

72
00:05:27.083 --> 00:05:30.239
Revenue sources create incentives and constraints.

73
00:05:30.239 --> 00:05:38.572
Sales activity, property values, service costs, pension obligations, restricted funds, grants, and capital schedules affect choices.

74
00:05:38.572 --> 00:05:42.108
A city cannot freely move every dollar to every purpose.

75
00:05:42.108 --> 00:05:44.444
A grant may define eligible projects.

76
00:05:44.444 --> 00:05:47.853
An enterprise fund has a specific financial structure.

77
00:05:47.853 --> 00:05:51.704
A one-time payment cannot safely support a permanent program.

78
00:05:51.704 --> 00:05:55.744
Ask which fund, which time horizon, and which legal restriction.

79
00:05:55.744 --> 00:05:57.133
Informal norms matter.

80
00:05:57.133 --> 00:05:59.911
Who gets called before a proposal is public?

81
00:05:59.911 --> 00:06:02.183
Which departments coordinate easily?

82
00:06:02.183 --> 00:06:06.665
Does the public expect commissioners to negotiate design from the dais?

83
00:06:06.665 --> 00:06:09.759
Are difficult choices postponed until a deadline?

84
00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:14.494
Norms can help institutions work, but they can also obscure accountability.

85
00:06:14.494 --> 00:06:20.302
Advanced governance analysis makes informal practice visible without assuming it is corrupt.

86
00:06:20.302 --> 00:06:23.016
Apply this to a Torrance corridor strategy.

87
00:06:23.016 --> 00:06:24.279
Create an actor map.

88
00:06:24.279 --> 00:06:39.935
Put the City Council, planning commission, city manager, relevant departments, city attorney, property owners, tenants, employers, neighborhood groups, transit and regional agencies, utilities, school institutions, and state regulators on the page.

89
00:06:39.935 --> 00:06:45.428
For each, list authority, resources, interests, veto points, and implementation duties.

90
00:06:45.428 --> 00:06:49.215
Suppose the goal is safer access and more housing near jobs.

91
00:06:49.215 --> 00:06:50.983
Planning may propose zoning.

92
00:06:50.983 --> 00:06:53.445
Public works may control street design.

93
00:06:53.445 --> 00:06:57.801
Transit service may belong to another agency or require coordination.

94
00:06:57.801 --> 00:07:00.263
Utilities may determine upgrade timing.

95
00:07:00.263 --> 00:07:02.410
Owners control project initiation.

96
00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:05.819
State law sets housing obligations and approval rules.

97
00:07:05.819 --> 00:07:09.038
The council controls local legislation and budgets.

98
00:07:09.038 --> 00:07:13.836
Residents and workers supply experience and political support or opposition.

99
00:07:13.836 --> 00:07:16.740
No single actor can deliver the outcome alone.

100
00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.017
Use the map to design strategy, not to assign blame.

101
00:07:20.017 --> 00:07:31.360
If a desired bus frequency lies outside direct city control, identify the agency with operational authority, the data it uses, the funding cycle, and the city's available leverage.

102
00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:40.183
If zoning capacity exists but projects do not occur, examine land assembly, financing, infrastructure, market conditions, and approval risk.

103
00:07:40.183 --> 00:07:46.926
If implementation stalls inside city government, locate the handoff, budget, staffing, or policy ambiguity.

104
00:07:46.926 --> 00:07:50.013
Accountability requires matching claims to power.

105
00:07:50.013 --> 00:07:56.189
Do not credit a body for an outcome it could not cause or condemn it for a power it does not hold.

106
00:07:56.189 --> 00:08:00.915
At the same time, lack of unilateral control is not lack of responsibility.

107
00:08:00.915 --> 00:08:07.910
Officials can convene, negotiate, disclose constraints, advocate to other levels, and design lawful incentives.

108
00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:09.801
Three recall questions follow.

109
00:08:09.801 --> 00:08:10.620
Question one.

110
00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:13.519
What five governance questions should you ask?

111
00:08:13.519 --> 00:08:13.960
Answer.

112
00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:23.602
Who has formal authority, who controls resources, who defines the problem, who can block or reshape implementation, and at what scale the issue operates.

113
00:08:23.602 --> 00:08:24.421
Question two.

114
00:08:24.421 --> 00:08:27.068
Why is an organization chart insufficient?

115
00:08:27.068 --> 00:08:27.509
Answer.

116
00:08:27.509 --> 00:08:36.646
It identifies formal roles but not agenda control, ownership, finance, information, mobilization, implementation, and cross-government influence.

117
00:08:36.646 --> 00:08:37.591
Question three.

118
00:08:37.591 --> 00:08:39.986
How should accountability be assigned?

119
00:08:39.986 --> 00:08:40.427
Answer.

120
00:08:40.427 --> 00:08:48.746
Match claims to actual authority and leverage, while recognizing duties to coordinate, explain, and advocate when control is shared.

121
00:08:48.746 --> 00:08:53.220
Your learner artifact is a governance actor map for one Torrance issue.

122
00:08:53.220 --> 00:09:01.979
Give every actor columns for formal power, practical influence, resources, incentives, dependencies, veto points, and implementation tasks.

123
00:09:01.979 --> 00:09:04.185
Draw arrows showing who needs whom.

124
00:09:04.185 --> 00:09:07.651
Mark each assertion as confirmed, inferred, or unknown.

125
